    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Thanks. You mentioned \"friction\" and the way roundabouts can smooth things out compared to the controlled metering of signals. Could you expand on what causes that \"friction\" in a signalized system? I'm looking for specific factors that cause these sudden disruptions or interruptions in the flow during peak periods.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Traffic friction in signalized systems primarily arises from the iterative stopping and starting inherent to red lights, which creates shockwaves through the network. Each required stop introduces acceleration and deceleration phases, wasting kinetic energy and increasing localized queuing. Furthermore, signal timing conflicts, such as undersized green times or poorly coordinated offsets, force more frequent unnecessary stops even when capacity exists. These abrupt changes in vehicle speed are the core contributors to flow instability and increased overall operational friction.",
      "source": "target",
